\section{An \code{ip6.arpa} name is always 32 labels}{


No \code{::}, no suppressed leading zeros, no mixed 4-in-6 spelling. \verb{::1} has 32
labels, 31 of them \code{0}. This is the opposite of the text form -- see
\code{\link[=addr_format]{addr_format()}} -- and it is why the name is built from the bits rather than
from the rendered address. An \verb{in-addr.arpa} name is always 4 labels, in
decimal, with leading zeros omitted (RFC 1035 §3.5: "leading zeros omitted
except in the case of a zero octet which is represented by a single zero").
}

\section{The trailing dot, and the case}{


The name is emitted \strong{fully qualified}, with the trailing dot that stands
for the root label (RFC 1035 §3.1). \verb{1.2.0.192.in-addr.arpa} and
\verb{1.2.0.192.in-addr.arpa.} denote the same name but are not the same string,
so raddr picks the unambiguous one.

Hex labels are lowercase. Comparison in the DNS is case-insensitive
(RFC 1035 §3.1: "Name servers and resolvers must compare labels in a
case-insensitive manner"), so \code{B.A.9} and \code{b.a.9} are the same name; the
lowercase choice follows RFC 5952 §4.3.
}

\section{The 4-in-6 form gets the mechanical answer}{


No RFC says whether \verb{::ffff:192.0.2.1} should map into \code{ip6.arpa} or into
\verb{1.2.0.192.in-addr.arpa.} raddr returns the \strong{\code{ip6.arpa}} name, because the
